#include <condition_variable>
#include <mutex>
#include <queue>

class ThreadSafeQueue {
    std::queue<int> q_;
    std::mutex m_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
public:
    void push(int v) {
        {   std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lk(m_);
            q_.push(v);              // shared state: under the mutex
        }
        cv_.notify_one();            // notification itself needs no lock
    }
    int pop() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m_);   // unique_lock: wait() unlocks it
        while (q_.empty()) cv_.wait(lk);       // re-test: spurious wakeups
        int v = q_.front();
        q_.pop();
        return v;                    // still holding the lock until lk destructs
    }
};
```
**Which parts need the mutex:** every access to `q_` - push, front, pop - because the predicate ("queue non-empty") and the data must change atomically together. **Which do not:** `notify_one()` can be called after releasing the lock; waking is not part of the invariant. **Why the loop:** `wait()` can return spuriously (no notification at all), and a notification may have been consumed before this thread started waiting; either way the queue can still be empty when we wake, so we must re-test and go back to sleep. With an `if`, a spurious wakeup calls `q_.front()` on an empty queue, undefined behaviour.

